CHUNK({"chunk":"A","module":"m1","topics":[{"id":"m1-evs-intro","title":"Environmental studies: definition, scope and importance","short":"What EVS is","blurb":"Environmental science vs environmental studies vs environmental engineering, the ten areas EVS covers, why it matters, and why it needs many subjects at once.","source":"Lecture W1S1, pages 1–5","tabs":[{"title":"Three definitions","html":"\n          <p class=\"lead\">Three names, three angles on the same problem: <b>science</b> studies it, <b>studies</b> adds society and policy, <b>engineering</b> builds the fix.</p>\n          <table class=\"tbl\"><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>What it is</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n            <tr><td><b>Environmental science</b></td><td>Study of the environment and the interactions between humans and natural systems, aiming to understand and solve environmental problems.</td></tr>\n            <tr><td><b>Environmental studies</b></td><td>Interdisciplinary field on how people and the environment interact, stressing the <b>ethical, social, political and economic</b> sides, to advance sustainable solutions.</td></tr>\n            <tr><td><b>Environmental engineering</b></td><td>Uses engineering and scientific principles to <b>preserve and enhance</b> environmental quality: waste management, pollution prevention, water and air purification, sustainable development.</td></tr>\n          </tbody></table>\n          <h3>Who said what</h3>\n          <ul>\n            <li><b>Encyclopedia Britannica</b>: environmental science is an interdisciplinary field drawing on ecology, geology, meteorology, biology, chemistry, engineering and physics to study environmental problems and human impacts.</li>\n            <li><b>Spellman &amp; Stoudt</b>: the interdisciplinary study of <b>how the Earth works</b> and how we are affecting the Earth's life support systems.</li>\n          </ul>\n          <p>Environmental studies takes the broader view: it covers both social and natural sciences, and shows how history, politics, public policy and law are tied to environmental issues. Environmental engineering, put simply, designs solutions for pollution, waste and climate change.</p>\n          <div class=\"note trap\"><p>Studies = people, ethics, policy. Science = how nature works. Engineering = technology and systems. Match the keyword to the field.</p></div>\n        "},{"title":"Scope","html":"\n          <p class=\"lead\">The scope of EVS is ten areas. Each pairs a job with the subject that does it.</p>\n          <table class=\"tbl\"><thead><tr><th>What is studied</th><th>Area</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n            <tr><td>Relationships between living things and their surroundings</td><td><b>Ecology and ecosystem studies</b></td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Weather patterns, air pollution, climate change</td><td><b>Atmospheric science</b></td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Geologic processes, soil, landforms, natural resources</td><td><b>Geoscience</b></td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Managing freshwater, water quality, conserving water</td><td><b>Hydrology and water resources</b></td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Sources and impacts of air, water and soil pollution, and solutions</td><td><b>Pollution control</b></td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Maintaining species and their natural environments</td><td><b>Biodiversity and conservation</b></td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Chemical processes in the environment and their impacts</td><td><b>Environmental chemistry</b></td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Legislation, rules and moral dilemmas of environmental protection</td><td><b>Environmental policy and management</b></td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Meeting current demands without endangering future generations</td><td><b>Sustainable development</b></td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Environmental effects of population growth and urban development</td><td><b>Human population and urbanization</b></td></tr>\n          </tbody></table>\n        "},{"title":"Importance","html":"\n          <p class=\"lead\">Seven reasons EVS matters. Most quiz questions on this tab just swap one reason for a wrong one.</p>\n          <ol>\n            <li>Helps us understand urgent global issues: <b>pollution, deforestation, climate change, biodiversity loss</b>.</li>\n            <li>Directs the <b>sustainable use</b> of natural resources, meeting today's demands without endangering future generations.</li>\n            <li>Stresses conserving and using <b>soil, water, air and energy</b> efficiently.</li>\n            <li>Helps <b>identify pollution sources</b> and develop technology and policies to reduce contamination.</li>\n            <li>Reduces the risk pollutants and harmful substances pose to <b>human health</b> by addressing environmental hazards.</li>\n            <li>Encourages <b>responsible behaviour</b> and environmental knowledge in individuals and communities.</li>\n            <li>Helps <b>predict and manage natural disasters</b> (floods, droughts, earthquakes) through better planning and preparedness.</li>\n          </ol>\n        "},{"title":"Multidisciplinary approach","html":"\n          <p class=\"lead\">Environmental problems are complex and interconnected, so no single subject can understand or solve them. A <b>multidisciplinary approach</b> integrates knowledge and methods from many disciplines.</p>\n          <p>EVS deals with every issue that affects an organism. It builds an appreciation of the natural world and of human impacts on it. It is an <b>applied science</b> because it looks for practical ways to make human civilisation sustainable on the Earth's <b>finite resources</b>.</p>\n          <table class=\"tbl\"><thead><tr><th>Discipline</th><th>Contribution</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n            <tr><td>Biology</td><td>Ecosystems, biodiversity, species interactions</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Chemistry</td><td>Pollution, chemical cycles (carbon, nitrogen), toxicity</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Physics</td><td>Energy flow, climate modelling, solar radiation</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Geology</td><td>Soil, minerals, natural disasters, landforms</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Geography</td><td>Mapping, spatial relationships, environmental planning</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Economics</td><td>Cost-benefit analysis, resource management, sustainable development</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Political science</td><td>Environmental laws, international treaties, policy-making</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Sociology</td><td>Human behaviour, cultural impact, public awareness</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Engineering</td><td>Pollution control technologies, renewable energy systems</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Ethics / philosophy</td><td>Moral responsibility, environmental justice</td></tr>\n          </tbody></table>\n        "},{"title":"Why many subjects","html":"\n          <p class=\"lead\">Six reasons the multidisciplinary approach is needed.</p>\n          <ol>\n            <li><b>Holistic understanding</b>: issues are interconnected (climate change affects agriculture, health, water), so you need natural and social sciences together.</li>\n            <li><b>Effective problem solving</b>: combines scientific, economic and political tools. Water scarcity may need <b>hydrology</b> (science), <b>policy</b> (law) and <b>infrastructure</b> (engineering).</li>\n            <li><b>Sustainable development</b>: balances ecological preservation, economic development and social equity, which needs input from many fields for long-term impact.</li>\n            <li><b>Informed decision-making</b>: governments, industries and individuals need broad-based knowledge to decide responsibly.</li>\n            <li><b>Policy formulation and implementation</b>: sound policy needs science, society, law and economics.</li>\n            <li><b>Adaptability to emerging challenges</b>: new threats such as <b>microplastics</b> and global pandemics linked to habitat loss need updated knowledge across fields.</li>\n          </ol>\n          <div class=\"note exam\"><p>For a \"why multidisciplinary\" answer, give the six headings and attach the water-scarcity example to problem solving.</p></div>\n        "}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Field that stresses the ethical, social, political and economic sides of environmental problems?","back":"<b>Environmental studies</b>."},{"front":"Spellman &amp; Stoudt define environmental science as…","back":"The interdisciplinary study of <b>how the Earth works</b> and how we are affecting the Earth's life support systems."},{"front":"Field that uses engineering and scientific principles to preserve and enhance environmental quality?","back":"<b>Environmental engineering</b>."}],"cardCount":10,"formulaCount":0},{"id":"m1-env-issues","title":"Major environmental issues","short":"Environmental issues","blurb":"Eight issues with their causes, effects and Indian examples, the global picture with its numbers, India's situation, and loss of biodiversity.","source":"Lecture W1S1, pages 5–11","tabs":[{"title":"Climate change, deforestation","html":"\n          <p class=\"lead\">Each issue is taught as <b>cause → effects → example</b>. Learn the example, it is the easiest mark.</p>\n          <h3>1. Climate change</h3>\n          <div class=\"kv\">\n            <div><span>Cause</span><b>Greenhouse gases</b> (CO<sub>2</sub>, CH<sub>4</sub>, N<sub>2</sub>O) from fossil fuel burning, deforestation, industrial processes</div>\n            <div><span>Example</span>IPCC: average global temperature up by about <b>1.1 °C</b> since pre-industrial times</div>\n          </div>\n          <ul>\n            <li>Rising global temperatures (<b>global warming</b>)</li>\n            <li>Melting ice caps and <b>rising sea levels</b></li>\n            <li>Severe weather: droughts, floods, hurricanes</li>\n            <li>Effects on biodiversity and farming</li>\n          </ul>\n          <h3>2. Deforestation</h3>\n          <div class=\"kv\">\n            <div><span>Cause</span>Logging, agriculture, urban expansion, mining</div>\n            <div><span>Example</span><b>Amazon rainforest</b> deforestation adds significantly to global carbon emissions</div>\n          </div>\n          <ul>\n            <li>Loss of biodiversity and habitats</li>\n            <li>Disruption of the <b>water cycle</b></li>\n            <li>More CO<sub>2</sub> because <b>carbon sinks</b> are lost</li>\n            <li>Soil erosion and <b>desertification</b></li>\n          </ul>\n        "},{"title":"Waste, air and water","html":"\n          <h3>3. Waste management</h3>\n          <div class=\"kv\">\n            <div><span>Types</span>Municipal solid waste, plastic waste, organic waste, landfill overflow</div>\n            <div><span>Example</span>CPCB (2021): India produces over <b>150,000 tonnes</b> of municipal solid waste each day</div>\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"vs\">\n            <div class=\"side\"><h4>Issues</h4><ul><li>Landfills overflowing</li><li><b>Methane</b> from decomposing organic material</li><li>Poor segregation and recycling</li></ul></div>\n            <div class=\"side\"><h4>Remedies</h4><ul><li><b>Reduce-Reuse-Recycle</b> (3Rs)</li><li>Composting and <b>waste-to-energy</b></li></ul></div>\n          </div>\n          <h3>4. Industrial and vehicular pollution</h3>\n          <div class=\"kv\">\n            <div><span>Industrial</span>Emissions, effluents, particulate matter (<b>PM2.5, PM10</b>)</div>\n            <div><span>Vehicular</span><b>CO, NOx, hydrocarbons</b> from combustion engines</div>\n            <div><span>Effects</span>Respiratory disease, smog, <b>acid rain</b>; contaminated air, water and soil</div>\n            <div><span>Example</span><b>Delhi</b> often ranks among the world's most polluted cities (vehicles + industry)</div>\n          </div>\n          <h3>5. Water pollution</h3>\n          <div class=\"kv\">\n            <div><span>Cause</span>Agricultural runoff with pesticides and fertilizers (<b>nitrates, phosphates</b>); industrial effluents and sewage</div>\n            <div><span>Effects</span><b>Eutrophication</b>, contaminated drinking water; cancer, reproductive issues, endocrine disruption</div>\n            <div><span>Example</span><b>Punjab</b> groundwater with pesticide residues like <b>Endosulfan and DDT</b></div>\n          </div>\n        "},{"title":"Hazardous, crackers, e-waste","html":"\n          <h3>6. Hazardous waste</h3>\n          <div class=\"vs\">\n            <div class=\"side\"><h4>a. Tannery sludge</h4><p>Cause: improper disposal of <b>chromium</b>-laden sludge from leather tanneries.</p><p>Effects: heavy metals in soil and groundwater; toxic to agriculture and health.</p><p>Example: <b>Ranipet (Tamil Nadu)</b>, severe chromium pollution in groundwater from old tannery dumps.</p></div>\n            <div class=\"side\"><h4>b. Biomedical waste</h4><p>Sources: hospitals, clinics, labs.</p><p>Hazards: infections (<b>HIV, hepatitis</b>), toxic chemicals, radioactive material.</p><p>Management: <b>segregation, incineration, autoclaving</b>.</p><p>Example: <b>COVID-19</b> caused a surge that strained disposal systems.</p></div>\n          </div>\n          <h3>7. Cracking down on crackers</h3>\n          <ul>\n            <li>Issue: heavy smoke, particulate matter and toxic gases (<b>SO<sub>2</sub>, NO, Pb</b>).</li>\n            <li>Effects: air pollution spikes during festivals (Diwali), respiratory distress in vulnerable people, <b>noise</b> that affects animals and the elderly.</li>\n            <li>Response: <b>Supreme Court</b> bans or limits crackers; <b>green crackers</b> promoted.</li>\n            <li>Example: Delhi's AQI reaches <b>\"Severe\"</b> after Diwali night every year.</li>\n          </ul>\n          <h3>8. E-waste</h3>\n          <ul>\n            <li>Sources: discarded mobiles, computers, TVs.</li>\n            <li>Hazards: <b>lead, mercury, cadmium</b> leach into soil and water; informal recycling harms workers.</li>\n            <li>Solutions: formal recycling centres, <b>extended producer responsibility (EPR)</b>.</li>\n            <li>Example: India is the <b>third-largest</b> e-waste generator, over <b>1 million tonnes/year</b> (CPCB, 2020).</li>\n          </ul>\n        "},{"title":"Global scenario","html":"\n          <p class=\"lead\">The international picture is five problems, each with one number to remember.</p>\n          <table class=\"tbl\"><thead><tr><th>Problem</th><th>Key fact</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n            <tr><td>Climate change</td><td>From fossil fuel GHGs; glacier melt, sea-level rise, extreme weather. Global action: <b>UNFCCC</b> and the <b>Paris Agreement (2015)</b> to limit warming <b>below 2 °C</b>; IPCC reports call for urgent action.</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Biodiversity loss</td><td><b>1 million species</b> at risk of extinction (deforestation, pollution, overfishing, habitat destruction)</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Plastic and marine pollution</td><td>Over <b>8 million tons</b> of plastic enter oceans each year; microplastics found in marine life and <b>human blood</b></td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Air pollution</td><td><b>99%</b> of the global population breathes unsafe air; smog in Beijing and Cairo</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>E-waste and resource depletion</td><td><b>53 million metric tons</b> of e-waste in 2020; mineral demand drives overextraction and geopolitical tension</td></tr>\n          </tbody></table>\n        "},{"title":"India and biodiversity loss","html":"\n          <p class=\"lead\">India's problems come from <b>population density, industrial growth and urban expansion</b>.</p>\n          <table class=\"tbl\"><thead><tr><th>Problem</th><th>India's numbers</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n            <tr><td>Air pollution</td><td><b>14 of the world's 20</b> most polluted cities; Delhi often severe (vehicles, <b>crop burning</b>, industry)</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Water pollution</td><td><b>70%</b> of surface water polluted, especially <b>Ganga and Yamuna</b>; pesticides in groundwater</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Deforestation and biodiversity</td><td>Forests lost to mining, farming, infrastructure; <b>10%</b> of flora and fauna at risk</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Waste management</td><td>Over <b>160,000 tonnes</b> of solid waste daily, about <b>60%</b> collected, under <b>30%</b> processed; e-waste over 1 million tonnes a year</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Climate vulnerability</td><td>Ranks <b>7th</b> in climate risk: floods, droughts, cyclones hitting agriculture</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Hazardous waste</td><td>Improper disposal of medical and industrial waste, e.g. chromium in <b>Ranipet</b></td></tr>\n          </tbody></table>\n          <div class=\"note heads-up\"><p>The notes give India's daily solid waste as over 150,000 tonnes (CPCB 2021, waste management issue) and over 160,000 tonnes (India scenario). Quote each figure in its own context.</p></div>\n          <h3>Loss of biodiversity</h3>\n          <p>The decline or disappearance of plant, animal and microorganism species from Earth. Main causes: <b>habitat destruction, pollution, climate change, overexploitation, invasive species</b>. It weakens ecosystems, disturbs food chains and reduces nature's ability to support life, including ours.</p>\n          <p>The lecture closes on the same point: these problems are woven from many threads, so a multidisciplinary approach gives integrated, sustainable solutions that balance ecological health with human needs.</p>\n        "}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Three greenhouse gases named as the cause of climate change?","back":"<b>CO<sub>2</sub>, CH<sub>4</sub>, N<sub>2</sub>O</b>."},{"front":"Global temperature rise since pre-industrial times (IPCC)?","back":"About <b>1.1 °C</b>."},{"front":"Example for deforestation?","back":"<b>Amazon rainforest</b>, a big contributor to global carbon emissions."}],"cardCount":12,"formulaCount":0},{"id":"m1-earth-spheres","title":"Earth as a life support system: atmosphere and hydrosphere","short":"Atmosphere & water","blurb":"The four spheres, what air is made of, the five layers of the atmosphere with their heights and temperatures, and the hydrosphere and water cycle.","source":"Lecture W1S3, pages 1–8","tabs":[{"title":"Four spheres","html":"\n          <p class=\"lead\">Earth supports life by supplying four things, each from one <b>sphere</b>. \"Sphere\" means <b>to surround or encompass</b>.</p>\n          <table class=\"tbl\"><thead><tr><th>Need</th><th>Sphere</th><th>Root word</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n            <tr><td>Air to breathe</td><td><b>Atmosphere</b></td><td>atmo → steam and vapour</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Water to drink</td><td><b>Hydrosphere</b></td><td>hydro → water</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Food sources</td><td><b>Biosphere</b></td><td>bio → life</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Solid ground and nutrients</td><td><b>Lithosphere</b></td><td>litho → rocks and minerals</td></tr>\n          </tbody></table>\n          <p>The lithosphere gives land and minerals, the hydrosphere water, the atmosphere air and weather. The <b>biosphere</b> holds every living thing and <b>depends on the other three</b>. Together they keep the environment stable and liveable.</p>\n          <figure data-fig=\"m1-four-spheres\"></figure>\n        "},{"title":"Air and its layers","html":"\n          <p class=\"lead\">The atmosphere is the <b>gaseous layer</b> around Earth: gases plus tiny water particles forming a thin protective envelope.</p>\n          <p>It provides oxygen, <b>shields life from harmful solar radiation</b>, regulates temperature, and drives weather and the water cycle.</p>\n          <div class=\"kv\">\n            <div><span>Nitrogen</span><b>78%</b> by volume</div>\n            <div><span>Oxygen</span><b>21%</b></div>\n            <div><span>Others</span><b>1%</b>: water vapour, CO<sub>2</sub>, methane (small amounts, but they matter for climate and energy balance)</div>\n          </div>\n          <figure data-fig=\"m1-air-composition\"></figure>\n          <p>Bottom to top, the layers are <b>troposphere → stratosphere → mesosphere → thermosphere → exosphere</b>.</p>\n          <figure data-fig=\"m1-atmosphere-layers-photo\"></figure>\n          <div class=\"mnemonic\"><p>\"<b>T</b>he <b>S</b>trong <b>M</b>an <b>T</b>hrows <b>E</b>ggs\": Tropo, Strato, Meso, Thermo, Exo.</p></div>\n        "},{"title":"Troposphere, stratosphere","html":"\n          <div class=\"vs\">\n            <div class=\"side\"><h4>Troposphere (0–15 km)</h4>\n              <ul>\n                <li><b>Lowest</b> layer, where all life exists; birds fly and airplanes operate here.</li>\n                <li>Holds <b>most of the atmosphere's mass</b> and almost all weather: rain, snow, storms, clouds, wind.</li>\n                <li>Warmest near the ground (heat radiates from the surface); temperature <b>falls with height</b>.</li>\n                <li>Height varies slightly with latitude and weather.</li>\n                <li>Top boundary: <b>tropopause</b>, a lid that keeps weather from rising into the stratosphere. Temperature levels off there before rising again.</li>\n              </ul></div>\n            <div class=\"side\"><h4>Stratosphere (15–50 km)</h4>\n              <ul>\n                <li>Second layer, holds the <b>ozone layer</b> (high ozone concentration).</li>\n                <li>Ozone absorbs most of the Sun's harmful <b>UV radiation</b>.</li>\n                <li>Temperature <b>rises with height</b> because ozone absorbs solar energy.</li>\n                <li>Air is very <b>stable and dry</b>: suits high-altitude aircraft and <b>weather balloons</b>.</li>\n                <li>Top boundary: <b>stratopause</b>.</li>\n              </ul></div>\n          </div>\n          <figure data-fig=\"m1-stratosphere-profile\"></figure>\n          <figure data-fig=\"m1-ozone-uv\"></figure>\n          <div class=\"note why\"><p>Temperature drops with height in the troposphere because the ground is the heater. In the stratosphere the heater is ozone absorbing UV, so it gets warmer the higher you go.</p></div>\n        "},{"title":"Mesosphere and above","html":"\n          <div class=\"vs\">\n            <div class=\"side\"><h4>Mesosphere (50–85 km)</h4>\n              <ul>\n                <li><b>Coldest</b> layer, down to about <b>−90 °C</b>.</li>\n                <li>Water vapour freezes into ice clouds called <b>noctilucent clouds</b>, seen at twilight, among the highest clouds.</li>\n                <li>Very low pressure and thin air, hard to study.</li>\n                <li><b>Meteors burn up</b> here from friction (shooting stars).</li>\n                <li>Top boundary: <b>mesopause</b>.</li>\n              </ul></div>\n            <div class=\"side\"><h4>Thermosphere</h4>\n              <ul>\n                <li>Above the mesosphere; temperature <b>rises with height</b> from absorbed energetic <b>UV and X-ray</b> radiation.</li>\n                <li>Upper part about <b>500 °C to 2,000 °C</b> or higher.</li>\n                <li><b>Auroras</b> (Northern and Southern Lights) occur here.</li>\n                <li>Satellites, spacecraft and the <b>International Space Station</b> orbit here.</li>\n              </ul></div>\n            <div class=\"side\"><h4>Exosphere</h4>\n              <ul>\n                <li><b>Final frontier</b>, the outermost layer.</li>\n                <li>Air constantly but gradually <b>leaks into space</b>; no clear upper boundary.</li>\n                <li>Extremely thin air; satellites orbit here too.</li>\n              </ul></div>\n          </div>\n          <figure data-fig=\"m1-mesosphere-diagram\"></figure>\n        "},{"title":"Layers at a glance","html":"\n          <p class=\"lead\">The summary table from the notes. Learn the boundaries and one feature per layer.</p>\n          <table class=\"tbl\"><thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Altitude (km)</th><th>Temperature (°C)</th><th>Feature</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n            <tr><td>Troposphere</td><td><b>0–15</b></td><td>15 to −56</td><td>Weather occurs here</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Stratosphere</td><td><b>15–50</b></td><td>−56 to −2</td><td>Ozone layer</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Mesosphere</td><td><b>50–85</b></td><td>−2 to −92</td><td>Meteors burn</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Thermosphere</td><td><b>85–800</b></td><td>−92 to 1200</td><td>Auroras</td></tr>\n          </tbody></table>\n          <figure data-fig=\"m1-atmosphere-poster\"></figure>\n          <div class=\"note heads-up\"><p>The numbers do not all agree. The text gives the upper thermosphere as 500–2,000 °C, the table tops out at 1200 °C, and the mesosphere is \"about −90 °C\" in the text but −92 °C in the table. The poster shows the troposphere as 0–12 km and the stratosphere as 12–50 km. For answers, use the text and table: troposphere <b>0–15 km</b>, stratosphere <b>15–50 km</b>, mesosphere <b>50–85 km</b>.</p></div>\n        "},{"title":"Hydrosphere and water cycle","html":"\n          <p class=\"lead\">The hydrosphere is <b>all the water</b> on Earth's surface, underground and in the air: liquid, ice and vapour.</p>\n          <div class=\"kv\">\n            <div><span>Oceans</span><b>~97%</b></div>\n            <div><span>Lakes and rivers</span>~2%</div>\n            <div><span>Freshwater usable by humans</span>only <b>1%</b> (rivers, lakes, streams, underground)</div>\n          </div>\n          <p>It sustains all life, <b>distributes heat</b> around the planet to regulate climate, and includes saltwater, freshwater, glaciers and ice caps, groundwater and water in the lower atmosphere. Its links with the atmosphere and biosphere keep ecosystems balanced.</p>\n          <div class=\"note heads-up\"><p>The notes say about 2% of water is in lakes and rivers. Standard figures put most of that 2–3% in glaciers and ice caps, with lakes and rivers far smaller. Write the notes' numbers in the exam.</p></div>\n          <h3>Water (hydrologic) cycle</h3>\n          <ol class=\"steps\">\n            <li><b>Evaporation</b> Sun's heat turns water from oceans, lakes and rivers into vapour.</li>\n            <li><b>Condensation</b> Vapour cools into droplets, forming clouds.</li>\n            <li><b>Precipitation</b> Heavy droplets fall as rain, snow, etc.</li>\n            <li><b>Runoff / infiltration</b> Water flows into rivers and streams or soaks into the ground.</li>\n            <li><b>Collection</b> It gathers in oceans or lakes and the cycle repeats.</li>\n          </ol>\n          <p>The cycle distributes water, supports life and shapes weather.</p>\n          <figure data-fig=\"m1-water-cycle-spheres\"></figure>\n        "}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What does \"sphere\" mean?","back":"To <b>surround or encompass</b>."},{"front":"Root meaning of \"atmo\"?","back":"<b>Steam and vapour</b>."},{"front":"Which sphere depends on the other three?","back":"The <b>biosphere</b>."}],"cardCount":12,"formulaCount":0},{"id":"m1-geosphere","title":"Geosphere","short":"Geosphere","blurb":"Earth's interior layers, rocks, minerals, soil and landforms, the processes that shape the surface, why the geosphere matters, and how it interacts with the other spheres.","source":"Lecture W1S3, pages 8–12","tabs":[{"title":"What it is and its layers","html":"\n          <p class=\"lead\">The geosphere is the <b>solid part</b> of Earth, from the <b>inner core to the crust</b>: rocks, minerals, soil, mountains, volcanoes and all landforms.</p>\n          <p>It is the foundation for land life and drives <b>plate tectonics, mountain formation and erosion</b>.</p>\n          <table class=\"tbl\"><thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>What it is</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n            <tr><td>Inner core</td><td><b>Solid</b>, mainly <b>iron and nickel</b></td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Outer core</td><td><b>Liquid</b>, responsible for Earth's <b>magnetic field</b></td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Mantle</td><td><b>Semi-solid</b> rock under the crust; its <b>convection currents</b> drive plate tectonics</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Crust</td><td>Outermost layer, with <b>continental and oceanic plates</b></td></tr>\n          </tbody></table>\n          <figure data-fig=\"m1-earth-layers\"></figure>\n        "},{"title":"Rocks, minerals, soil, landforms","html":"\n          <div class=\"kv\">\n            <div><span>Rocks</span>Naturally occurring solid aggregates of one or more minerals or mineraloids. Three types by origin: <b>igneous, sedimentary, metamorphic</b>.</div>\n            <div><span>Minerals</span>Naturally occurring, <b>inorganic</b> solids with a <b>definite chemical composition</b> and a <b>crystalline structure</b>.</div>\n            <div><span>Soil</span>Thin surface layer formed by <b>weathering of rocks</b> and <b>decay of organic matter</b>. Contains minerals, organic matter, water and air; supports plants and nutrient cycling.</div>\n            <div><span>Landforms</span>Mountains, valleys, plateaus, volcanoes, faults. Formed by <b>tectonic processes</b> (earthquakes, eruptions, plate movement) together with erosion and weathering over long periods.</div>\n          </div>\n          <h3>Geological processes</h3>\n          <ol>\n            <li><b>Plate tectonics</b>: moving crustal plates cause earthquakes, volcanic eruptions and mountain building.</li>\n            <li><b>Weathering and erosion</b>: rocks are broken down and carried away by wind, water or ice.</li>\n            <li><b>Sedimentation</b>: sediments pile up in layers and harden into sedimentary rock.</li>\n          </ol>\n        "},{"title":"Importance and interactions","html":"\n          <h3>Why the geosphere matters</h3>\n          <ul>\n            <li><b>Supports ecosystems</b>: soil for plants, habitats for animals.</li>\n            <li><b>Source of natural resources</b>: minerals, metals, fossil fuels for energy, construction, manufacturing.</li>\n            <li><b>Affects climate</b>: volcanic gases and ash can cool or warm the atmosphere.</li>\n            <li><b>Determines physical geography</b>: landforms set elevation, terrain and where people settle.</li>\n          </ul>\n          <h3>Interactions with other spheres</h3>\n          <p>The geosphere constantly interacts with the atmosphere, hydrosphere, biosphere and <b>cryosphere</b>, making Earth a dynamic system.</p>\n          <table class=\"tbl\"><thead><tr><th>Sphere</th><th>Interaction</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n            <tr><td>Atmosphere</td><td>Volcanic eruptions release <b>CO<sub>2</sub> and ash</b>, affecting air quality and climate; wind and rain weather rocks in return.</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Hydrosphere</td><td>Rivers <b>erode landforms</b> and carry sediment, shaping valleys and coasts; groundwater moving through rock affects erosion and mineral distribution.</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Biosphere</td><td><b>Roots break rocks</b> and add organic matter to soil; burrowing animals change soil structure.</td></tr>\n          </tbody></table>\n        "}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Composition and state of the inner core?","back":"<b>Solid</b>, mainly <b>iron and nickel</b>."},{"front":"Which layer produces Earth's magnetic field?","back":"The <b>liquid outer core</b>."},{"front":"What drives plate tectonics?","back":"<b>Convection currents</b> in the semi-solid mantle."}],"cardCount":8,"formulaCount":0},{"id":"m1-biosphere","title":"Biosphere","short":"Biosphere","blurb":"What the biosphere is, its living components and ecosystems, its functions, and how living things drive the carbon, nitrogen, oxygen and phosphorus cycles.","source":"Lecture W1S3, pages 12–14","tabs":[{"title":"Components and ecosystems","html":"\n          <p class=\"lead\">The biosphere is the <b>global sum of all ecosystems</b>: every living organism and the environments it interacts with, from soil bacteria to the largest whales, across land, water and air.</p>\n          <figure data-fig=\"m1-biosphere-diagram\"></figure>\n          <h3>Living organisms</h3>\n          <div class=\"kv\">\n            <div><span>Plants</span><b>Primary producers</b>: turn sunlight into energy by photosynthesis; the base of food chains</div>\n            <div><span>Animals</span><b>Consumers</b>: herbivores eat plants; carnivores and omnivores eat other animals</div>\n            <div><span>Microorganisms</span>Bacteria, fungi, algae and other microbes <b>decompose</b> dead matter, recycle nutrients, build soil fertility</div>\n          </div>\n          <figure data-fig=\"m1-food-chain-cycle\"></figure>\n          <h3>Ecosystems</h3>\n          <p>An ecosystem is a community of living organisms (<b>biotic</b>) interacting with non-living parts (<b>abiotic</b>: sunlight, soil, air, water). Examples in the biosphere:</p>\n          <div class=\"chips\"><span class=\"chip\">Forests</span><span class=\"chip\">Grasslands</span><span class=\"chip\">Deserts</span><span class=\"chip\">Freshwater (rivers, lakes)</span><span class=\"chip\">Marine (oceans, coral reefs)</span></div>\n          <p>Each supports different forms and levels of biodiversity.</p>\n        "},{"title":"Functions and cycles","html":"\n          <p class=\"lead\">Three functions: <b>supports life</b> (food, water, oxygen, habitat), <b>maintains biodiversity</b> (more species, more resilience to disruption), and <b>regulates biogeochemical cycles</b>.</p>\n          <p>Living things drive the flow of <b>carbon, nitrogen, oxygen and phosphorus</b>.</p>\n          <figure data-fig=\"m1-biogeochemical-components\"></figure>\n          <table class=\"tbl\"><thead><tr><th>Cycle</th><th>How the biosphere runs it</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n            <tr><td>Carbon</td><td>Plants take in <b>CO<sub>2</sub></b> in photosynthesis and store carbon; animals eat plants; breathing, decomposition and burning (fossil fuels) release it to air or soil.</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Nitrogen</td><td>Bacteria in soil and roots convert <b>atmospheric nitrogen</b> into forms plants use; it moves up the food chain; other microbes return it to the air by <b>denitrification</b>.</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Oxygen</td><td>Plants make O<sub>2</sub> in photosynthesis; animals use it and release CO<sub>2</sub>, which plants reuse. A balanced oxygen–carbon loop.</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Phosphorus</td><td>Organisms take phosphorus from soil and use it in <b>DNA and energy transfer</b>; decomposers return it to the soil.</td></tr>\n          </tbody></table>\n        "}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Definition of the biosphere?","back":"The <b>global sum of all ecosystems</b> where life exists on Earth."},{"front":"Role of plants in the biosphere?","back":"<b>Primary producers</b>; base of food chains."},{"front":"Role of microorganisms in the biosphere?","back":"<b>Decompose</b> dead matter, recycle nutrients, add to soil fertility."}],"cardCount":6,"formulaCount":0}],"figures":[{"id":"m1-four-spheres","src":"pages/m1/evs-w1-02/p11.png","crop":[0.1,0.48,0.52,0.355],"caption":"Earth sits in the middle of the four spheres: atmosphere, hydrosphere, biosphere and lithosphere.","alt":"Circle divided into four photo segments labelled atmosphere, hydrosphere, biosphere and lithosphere around a picture of Earth."},{"id":"m1-air-composition","src":"pages/m1/evs-w1-02/p02.png","crop":[0.25,0.14,0.55,0.22],"caption":"Nitrogen is the big slice (78%), oxygen next (21%), and everything else is a sliver (1%).","alt":"Pie chart of air composition with nitrogen, oxygen and others."},{"id":"m1-atmosphere-layers-photo","src":"pages/m1/evs-w1-02/p02.png","crop":[0.13,0.53,0.79,0.35],"caption":"The five layers stacked above Earth, troposphere at the bottom and exosphere at the top.","alt":"Photo of Earth from space with bands labelled troposphere, stratosphere, mesosphere, thermosphere and exosphere."},{"id":"m1-stratosphere-profile","src":"pages/m1/evs-w1-02/p03.png","crop":[0.1,0.64,0.8,0.35],"caption":"Weather and clouds stay below the tropopause; the ozone layer, weather balloons and spy planes sit in the stratosphere; the red line shows temperature turning upward above the tropopause.","alt":"Altitude chart from the troposphere to the mesosphere with a temperature curve, aircraft, balloon, ozone layer and meteors."},{"id":"m1-ozone-uv","src":"pages/m1/evs-w1-02/p04.png","crop":[0.1,0.178,0.79,0.322],"caption":"Stratospheric ozone stops most incoming UV; surface-level ozone near cities is smog.","alt":"Diagram of UV-A, UV-B and UV-C rays reaching the stratospheric ozone layer above the troposphere."},{"id":"m1-mesosphere-diagram","src":"pages/m1/evs-w1-02/p05.png","crop":[0.1,0.133,0.8,0.347],"caption":"Troposphere, stratosphere with the ozone layer, and mesosphere, separated by the tropopause, stratopause and mesopause.","alt":"Curved bands showing troposphere, stratosphere including ozone layer and mesosphere, with UV and methane molecules."},{"id":"m1-atmosphere-poster","src":"pages/m1/evs-w1-02/p06.png","crop":[0.1,0.29,0.79,0.445],"caption":"Each layer with what you find there: planes in the troposphere, the ozone layer in the stratosphere, meteors in the mesosphere, satellites and auroras higher up.","alt":"Poster of the atmosphere layers with heights and objects in each layer."},{"id":"m1-water-cycle-spheres","src":"pages/m1/evs-w1-02/p08.png","crop":[0.17,0.18,0.62,0.32],"caption":"Evaporation, condensation, precipitation, runoff and collection make one loop.","alt":"Water cycle illustration with evaporation, condensation, precipitation, runoff and collection labels."},{"id":"m1-earth-layers","src":"pages/m1/evs-w1-02/p09.png","crop":[0.1,0.07,0.8,0.36],"caption":"From the outside in: crust, mantle, outer core and inner core.","alt":"Cut-away of Earth showing crust, mantle, outer core and inner core."},{"id":"m1-biosphere-diagram","src":"pages/m1/evs-w1-02/p12.png","crop":[0.12,0.34,0.53,0.37],"caption":"The biosphere overlaps the other three spheres: life is found in air, water and earth.","alt":"Illustration of the biosphere with labelled zones for atmosphere, hydrosphere and lithosphere."},{"id":"m1-food-chain-cycle","src":"pages/m1/evs-w1-02/p13.png","crop":[0.1,0.13,0.43,0.34],"caption":"Producer to primary, secondary, tertiary and final consumers, with fungi returning matter to the start.","alt":"Circular food chain from green plant through grasshopper, frog, snake and hawk, with fungi."},{"id":"m1-biogeochemical-components","src":"pages/m1/evs-w1-02/p14.png","crop":[0.1,0.178,0.55,0.268],"caption":"The biosphere sits on top of five cycles: carbon, phosphorus, nitrogen, water and oxygen.","alt":"Graphic of the biosphere above five columns labelled carbon, phosphorus, nitrogen, water and oxygen cycle."}]});
